  "notes": " Each figure is the vendor's. https://dl.ui.com/ds/usw-enterprise-24-poe_ds.pdf (HTTP 200, application/pdf, 397,353 B, 2 pp) prints \"Networking interface (12) 10/100/1000 MbE RJ45 / (12) 100/1000/2500 MbE RJ45 / (2) 1/10G SFP+\", \"PoE interface (24) PoE/PoE+ (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-)\", \"Max. PoE+ wattage per port by PSE 34.2W\" and \"PoE budget 400W\"; https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-enterprise-24-poe (HTTP 200, 650,004 B, rendered spec table, read 2026-11-21) prints the same counts as rows (\"1 GbE RJ45 = 12 (All PoE+) ... / 2.5 GbE RJ45 = 12 (All PoE+) ...\", \"PoE+ = 24\", \"Total PoE Availability = 400W\"). Before this correction no copper port on this row declared poeOut. The engine's isPoePse() heuristic still gave each one a PSE, but at the undeclared 802.3af default of 15.4 W (engine/media.js, `if (port.poeOut === null ...) port.poeOut = 15.4`), so the faceplate tooltip and the port detail panel reported \"PoE out 15.4 W\" on a port the datasheet rates 802.3at at 34.2 W by PSE - the one figure an operator reads when asking whether the socket can power a 25.5 W AP. All 24 copper ports now declare the vendor's per-port PSE ceiling (34.2 W) and the vendor's class word (\"PoE/PoE+\"), and the row declares poe-pse/802.3af/802.3at instead of leaving the per-port figure to the undeclared-port default. The SFP+ cages are not PSE ports and stay undeclared."
